#!/usr/bin/env bash
# mesh-convexity — the SECOND-ORDER test the mesh applies too loosely.
# smoke-test: --test asserts leptokurtic→CONVEX, bounded→ROBUST, Cauchy→κ-UNRELIABLE, left-spikes→LEFT-HEAVY, mutation-verified.
#
# LITERATURE (live review) 2026-07-24, antifragility / convexity / ruin (Taleb), angle:
# "a foundational idea we may have MISread or applied too loosely."
#
# THE MISREAD: across CLAUDE.md we say "antifragile" to mean self-healing / resilient. But
# Taleb's formal definition — and its 2026 operationalization for multi-agent LLM systems — is
# CONVEXITY, a strictly SECOND-ORDER property, not resilience of the MEAN. A robust system has a
# FLAT response to dispersion; an antifragile one has a CONVEX (accelerating-upward) response;
# a fragile one CONCAVE. Every mesh gate today decides on a FIRST moment — load1>11 → shed,
# burn<7/h → cold-shrink, score>55 → grind, WER mean → pick model. None looks at the second
# moment / tail convexity. "Antifragile" in our doctrine is a first-order word for a
# second-order thing — applied too loosely.
#
# THE CONCEPT WE DID NOT EMBODY (cited): CAFE — "Cognitive Antifragility Framework for
# Evaluation" (Detecting Antifragility-Compatible Regimes in Multi-Agent LLM Systems,
# arXiv:2605.02463, 2026). Its detector is a *distributional Jensen Gap under a convex stress
# potential*: reconstruct the observed dispersion of a signal, compare it to a controlled
# EXPECTED distribution, and read the Jensen gap  E[f(X)] − f(E[X])  (f convex). A POSITIVE gap
# is a "convex-expansive deformation" — the signal exposes learnable stress structure — and the
# paper's key finding is that this can COEXIST with the mean getting WORSE (quality fell ~1/3
# under stress, yet every architecture showed a positive gap). So the mean-degradation our
# reflexes react to is NOT the whole story; the second-order geometry is a separate, measurable
# axis, and we had no instrument for it. This is that instrument.
#
# MECHANISM (faithful, minimal): given a numeric series X (one metric's samples),
#   z = standardize(X)                          (μ=0, σ=1; σ=0 ⇒ flat ⇒ robust by definition)
#   f = cosh  (SYMMETRIC convex potential — weights BOTH tails, so it measures dispersion-
#              convexity independent of skew; Taleb: fragility = sensitivity to a semi-measure
#              of DISPERSION, not to direction)
#   G_obs = mean(cosh(z)) − cosh(0)             the observed distributional Jensen gap
#   G_ref = E[cosh(N(0,1))] − 1 = e^0.5 − 1     the CONTROLLED expected distribution: a Gaussian
#                                               of MATCHED variance (the "no-excess-tail" null)
#   Δ = G_obs − G_ref, with a bootstrap 95% CI (as in CAFE: "bootstrap CIs above zero")
# VERDICT:
#   Δ CI wholly > 0 → CONVEX  (leptokurtic / fat-tailed: convex-expansive stress geometry —
#                              the antifragility-COMPATIBLE regime the paper detects; equally,
#                              the fragile-to-large-deviations regime — convexity is the shared
#                              second-order signature, sign of the effect is a separate question)
#   Δ CI wholly < 0 → ROBUST  (sub-Gaussian / bounded: dispersion does NOT expand convexly)
#   CI straddles 0  → GAUSSIAN-LIKE (no second-order excess either way)
# This is NOT an antifragile learner — like CAFE it is a MEASUREMENT LAYER: it says WHERE the
# second-order structure lives so a mind knows where convexity is even worth acting on.
#
# κ PRE-CHECK (reliability qualifier, not a new verdict) — LITERATURE 2026-07-28, angle: "the metric
# this area uses to measure ITSELF". The verdict standardizes X by its sample σ; that is only valid if
# the SECOND moment has CONVERGED on this window. On a fat-tailed axis σ is itself pre-asymptotic, so
# the CONVEX/ROBUST call rests on an unstable denominator — and until now nothing said so. κ (Taleb,
# "How Much Data Do You Need?", arXiv:1802.05495; folded into Statistical Consequences of Fat Tails,
# arXiv:2001.10488) measures exactly this from the samples in hand: M(b)=E|S_b−b·mean|, the mean-abs-dev
# of a SUM of b iid draws, scales as b^β — Gaussian β=½ (averaging shrinks the tail), Cauchy β=1 (it
# does not). κ=2−1/β ∈ [0,1]: 0=Gaussian-thin, 1=Cauchy-fat. It is the MULTIPLIER on sample size the LLN
# needs, so high κ ⇒ n here is too few for σ to mean anything. We estimate β by bootstrapping M(b) over
# block sizes b=1,2,4,… and regressing ln M(b) on ln b (reuses the CI bootstrap's RNG/seed). κ QUALIFIES
# the verdict, never overrules it: κ≲0.25 → (σ-stable); ≲0.5 → (σ pre-asymptotic — verdict tentative);
# else → (fat-tailed: n likely too few — σ-standardized gap UNRELIABLE). The convexity call still prints;
# a mind now knows whether to trust it. (Follow-on the steward may take: run this κ over a records.log axis
# to flag WHICH mesh-series-stats corpus figures are reported off windows too short to have converged.)
#
#   mesh-convexity < series.txt          read one number per line (or whitespace-sep) from stdin
#   mesh-convexity --lambda L            potential steepness (default 1.0; larger = more tail-weight)
#   mesh-convexity --test                smoke test (synthetic fat-tailed vs bounded, mutation-verified)
#   MESH_CVX_LAMBDAS=a,b,c               λ-sweep MULTIPLIERS for the threshold-stability gate below
#                                        (default 0.5,0.7071,1.0,1.4142,2.0 — a neighbourhood of λ)
#
# 2-shock note: for a RESPONSE CURVE y=f(x) (dose→response), Taleb-Douady's fast-and-frugal test is
# f(x−Δ)+f(x+Δ) ⋛ 2·f(x) (concave=fragile / convex=antifragile). This tool implements the
# DISTRIBUTIONAL gap (CAFE), the variant that needs only one signal's samples, no response curve.
#
# TAIL-DIRECTION AXIS — LITERATURE (live review) 2026-07-30, antifragility/convexity/ruin (Taleb),
# angle: an OPERATIONAL mechanism not yet embodied. THE GAP: the CAFE gap above uses a SYMMETRIC
# potential (cosh weights both tails), so it can say CONVEX/ROBUST but is BLIND to WHICH tail carries
# the convex weight — the code above literally prints "sign of the effect is a separate question". But
# Taleb-Douady's foundational definition (Mathematical Definition, Mapping, and Detection of
# (Anti)Fragility, arXiv:1208.1189; the "fragility below level K" result, restated in (Anti)Fragility
# and Convex Responses in Medicine, arXiv:1808.00065 §2 and Entropy 25(2):343 / arXiv:2209.14631) is
# EXPLICITLY one-sided: fragility is the sensitivity of the LEFT-tail partial expectation ∫_{-∞}^{K} to
# a mean-preserving increase in scale σ — the gap between ∫_K f(x,σ) and ∫_K f(x,σ+Δ), proportional to
# the CONCAVITY of f BELOW K. Fragility lives in ONE tail; a symmetric leptokurtic series (convex both
# sides) is NOT fragile, yet the CAFE verdict cannot tell it apart from a left-concave one. THE
# MECHANISM (model-free, same samples): split the standardized potential at z=0 and take the
# left−right half-gap A = mean_{z<0}[cosh(λz)−1] − mean_{z>0}[cosh(λz)−1], bootstrap its CI on the
# SAME resamples as Δ. A CI>0 ⇒ LEFT-HEAVY (down-tail convexity dominates), <0 ⇒ RIGHT-HEAVY, straddle
# ⇒ SYMMETRIC. It is report-only and orientation-agnostic (which tail, not whether that tail is harm —
# the consumer maps down/up→harm), the raw ingredient of Taleb's fragility SIGN that the symmetric gap
# discards. Fills the "LOCAL-vs-TAIL / directional fragility" gap left open by the κ + Parisian-ruin
# landings (docs/reviews/antifragility-parisian-ruin-dwell-resource-guard-2026-07-29.md).
#
# THRESHOLD-STABILITY GATE — LITERATURE (live review) 2026-08-10, antifragility/convexity/ruin (Taleb),
# angle: a known CRITIQUE / failure mode of the area. Cite: Luca Zhou (Sapienza), "Tail-Shape Estimation
# in LLM Evaluation Is Fragile: A Protocol for Diagnosing False Positives", arXiv:2606.16511v2 (1 Jul
# 2026). The paper pre-registers four gatekeeping requirements any POSITIVE tail-shape claim must clear
# (mean-equivalence · tail-magnitude equivalence · exceedance sample size nexc≥N* · GPD Anderson-Darling
# goodness-of-fit) plus a THRESHOLD-STABILITY gate, and on a real corpus it catches THREE modes of false
# positive that a naive analysis would have published — among them the "threshold-fishing trap": a
# single-threshold PASS at q=0.97 that lies INSIDE the model's own noise envelope and is REJECTED once
# ξ̂ is required to hold across q∈[0.95,0.99]. Its verdict on its own domain is negative: the tail-shape
# parameter added no discriminative information beyond bulk statistics.
#
# THE FAILURE MODE HERE, EXACTLY: λ is this tool's threshold. It is the free knob that decides how much
# weight the tail gets (`--lambda`, "larger = more tail-weight"), and BOTH published claims — the
# CONVEX/ROBUST/GAUSSIAN-LIKE verdict and the LEFT/RIGHT/SYMMETRIC direction — are read off ONE
# arbitrary λ=1.0 with nothing anywhere checking that they survive a neighbourhood of it. The κ
# pre-check guards a DIFFERENT thing (has the σ in the DENOMINATOR converged); it says nothing about the
# λ in the numerator. And the dependence is not cosmetic: the reference gap G_ref=exp(λ²/2)−1 grows
# super-exponentially in λ while G_obs is dominated by the few largest |z| the window happens to hold,
# so the bootstrap CI widens with λ and a thin-evidence CONVEX evaporates into GAUSSIAN-LIKE. MEASURED
# by this tool at its shipped B=2000 (both fixtures are in --test):
#   n=80, 5% spikes σ=5 (seed 11) → CONVEX at λ=0.5/0.71/1.0/1.41, GAUSSIAN-LIKE at λ=2.0
#   n=60, 3% spikes σ=6 (seed 21) → the DIRECTION claim RIGHT-HEAVY exists only at λ≥1.0; SYMMETRIC below
# Same data, same code, opposite claim; the default λ was simply the lucky one. NOTE the second fixture
# prints κ≈0.20 "(σ-stable — the standardized gap is trustworthy)" with a direction CI of [−1.92, −0.002]
# — κ certifies the DENOMINATOR and cheerfully passes a claim the λ-sweep destroys. The two qualifiers
# are ORTHOGONAL; neither substitutes for the other.
#
# THE MECHANISM (faithful transfer of the threshold gate, report-only): re-run the SAME bootstrap
# verdict over a MULTIPLICATIVE neighbourhood of the anchor λ (default ×{0.5, 0.71, 1, 1.41, 2}, the
# analogue of the paper's q-sweep; env MESH_CVX_LAMBDAS) and require BOTH verdicts to hold across it.
#   every λ agrees with the anchor      → stability: STABLE            (claim survives its own knob)
#   any λ flips either verdict          → stability: THRESHOLD-FRAGILE (named: which λ, to what)
# It NEVER overrules the headline — like κ it is a qualifier, so a mind reading CONVEX knows whether
# that word is a property of the data or of the default. Scale-free companion figure r(λ)=G_obs/G_ref
# (Δ itself is not comparable across λ — its units explode with the potential). Its own bootstrap RNG
# (seed+1), created after κ, so every pre-existing number in the output is byte-identical.
set -euo pipefail

usage(){ sed -n '2,/^[^#]/p' "$0" | sed -n 's/^# \?//p'; }
case "${1:-}" in -h|--help) usage; exit 0 ;; esac

LAMBDA="1.0"
if [ "${1:-}" = "--test" ]; then MODE="test"; shift || true; else MODE="run"; fi
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --lambda) shift; LAMBDA="${1:-1.0}" ;;
    --test)   MODE="test" ;;
    *) echo "unknown arg: $1 (see --help)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
  esac
  shift
done

_compute(){   # stdin: numeric series → verdict. env: LAMBDA, MESH_CVX_SEED
  # NB: `python3 - <<PY` takes stdin for the SCRIPT, so the series cannot also come on stdin —
  # consume it here with `cat` and hand it to python via env (MESH_CVX_DATA).
  local _data; _data="$(cat)"
  LAMBDA="$1" MESH_CVX_DATA="$_data" python3 - <<'PY'
import sys, os, math, random

lam = float(os.environ.get("LAMBDA", "1.0"))
seed = int(os.environ.get("MESH_CVX_SEED", "1729"))   # fixed seed ⇒ deterministic bootstrap/--test

raw = os.environ.get("MESH_CVX_DATA", "").split()
xs = []
for t in raw:
    try:
        xs.append(float(t))
    except ValueError:
        continue

n = len(xs)
if n < 8:
    print(f"convexity: n/a (need ≥8 samples, got {n})")
    sys.exit(2)

def gap(sample, lam):
    """Distributional Jensen gap of a sample under f=cosh(lam·z), standardized."""
    m = sum(sample) / len(sample)
    var = sum((x - m) ** 2 for x in sample) / len(sample)
    sd = math.sqrt(var)
    if sd == 0:
        return 0.0
    z = [(x - m) / sd for x in sample]
    g_obs = sum(math.cosh(lam * zi) for zi in z) / len(z) - math.cosh(0.0)
    return g_obs

def asymmetry(sample, lam):
    """Left−right tail-convexity asymmetry — Taleb-Douady fragility DIRECTION (arXiv:1208.1189).
    The symmetric cosh gap above weights BOTH tails equally, so it is blind to WHICH tail carries
    the convex weight and explicitly punts the sign. Taleb-Douady define fragility as the
    sensitivity of the LEFT-tail partial expectation (below a level K) to a mean-preserving spread —
    it lives in ONE tail. Split the SAME standardized potential at z=0 into its down/up halves:
      A = (1/n)Σ_{z<0}[cosh(λz)−1] − (1/n)Σ_{z>0}[cosh(λz)−1]
    A>0 ⇒ convex weight concentrated in the DOWN tail (large negative deviations dominate);
    A<0 ⇒ in the UP tail. Report-only: which tail, not whether that tail is harm — the metric is
    orientation-agnostic, so the consumer maps down/up→harm (as the CAFE verdict already punts sign)."""
    m = sum(sample) / len(sample)
    var = sum((x - m) ** 2 for x in sample) / len(sample)
    sd = math.sqrt(var)
    if sd == 0:
        return 0.0
    z = [(x - m) / sd for x in sample]
    n = len(z)
    gL = sum(math.cosh(lam * zi) - 1.0 for zi in z if zi < 0) / n
    gR = sum(math.cosh(lam * zi) - 1.0 for zi in z if zi > 0) / n
    return gL - gR

def kappa_estimate(sample, rng, reps=700):
    """Taleb's κ pre-asymptotic fat-tailedness metric (arXiv:1802.05495) from the samples in hand.
    M(b) = E|S_b − b·mean| = mean-abs-dev of a SUM of b iid draws (bootstrapped from the window).
    For iid sums M(b) ∝ b^β: Gaussian β=½ (averaging shrinks the tail), Cauchy β=1 (it does not).
    κ = 2 − 1/β  ⇒  Gaussian κ=0, Cauchy κ=1. κ is the multiplier on sample size the LLN needs, so a
    high κ means the second moment this verdict standardizes by is pre-asymptotic on this window."""
    N = len(sample)
    m = sum(sample) / N
    # block sizes b = 1,2,4,… up to ~N/2 (need distinct sums; b→N degenerates to one resample)
    bs = []
    b = 1
    while b <= max(2, N // 2):
        bs.append(b); b *= 2
    lb, lm = [], []
    for b in bs:
        acc = 0.0
        for _ in range(reps):
            s = sum(sample[rng.randrange(N)] for _ in range(b))
            acc += abs(s - b * m)
        Mb = acc / reps
        if Mb <= 0:
            continue
        lb.append(math.log(b)); lm.append(math.log(Mb))
    if len(lb) < 2:
        return None
    k = len(lb)
    mbx = sum(lb) / k; mby = sum(lm) / k
    den = sum((x - mbx) ** 2 for x in lb)
    if den == 0:
        return None
    beta = sum((lb[i] - mbx) * (lm[i] - mby) for i in range(k)) / den
    if beta <= 0:            # M(b) not growing ⇒ degenerate; treat as maximally fat
        return 1.0
    return max(0.0, min(1.0, 2.0 - 1.0 / beta))

g_obs = gap(xs, lam)
# Controlled expected distribution: Gaussian of matched variance. After standardizing, that is
# N(0,1); E[cosh(lam·N(0,1))] = exp(lam^2/2). The reference gap is exp(lam^2/2) − 1.
g_ref = math.exp(lam * lam / 2.0) - 1.0
delta = g_obs - g_ref

# Bootstrap 95% CI of Δ (CAFE: "bootstrap confidence intervals above zero"). The SAME resamples
# carry the left−right asymmetry A (Taleb-Douady fragility direction), so it costs no extra draws.
rng = random.Random(seed)
B = 2000
deltas = []
asyms = []
for _ in range(B):
    resample = [xs[rng.randrange(n)] for _ in range(n)]
    deltas.append(gap(resample, lam) - g_ref)
    asyms.append(asymmetry(resample, lam))
deltas.sort()
lo = deltas[int(0.025 * B)]
hi = deltas[int(0.975 * B)]
asyms.sort()
a_obs = asymmetry(xs, lam)
a_lo = asyms[int(0.025 * B)]
a_hi = asyms[int(0.975 * B)]
if a_lo > 0:
    a_verdict = "LEFT-HEAVY"
    a_gloss = "convex weight in the DOWN tail — large negative deviations dominate (if down=harm ⇒ FRAGILE)"
elif a_hi < 0:
    a_verdict = "RIGHT-HEAVY"
    a_gloss = "convex weight in the UP tail — gains-from-large-deviation direction (if up=benefit ⇒ ANTIFRAGILE)"
else:
    a_verdict = "SYMMETRIC"
    a_gloss = "both tails balanced — the symmetric verdict above is the whole story (no fragility direction)"

if lo > 0:
    verdict = "CONVEX"
    gloss = "leptokurtic / fat-tailed — convex-expansive stress geometry (antifragility-compatible & fragile-to-large-deviations share this 2nd-order signature)"
elif hi < 0:
    verdict = "ROBUST"
    gloss = "sub-Gaussian / bounded — dispersion does not expand convexly"
else:
    verdict = "GAUSSIAN-LIKE"
    gloss = "no second-order excess either way (CI straddles 0)"

# κ pre-check: is the σ this verdict standardizes by even converged on this window? (reuse rng/seed)
kappa = kappa_estimate(xs, rng)
if kappa is None:
    qual = ""
    kline = "  κ: n/a (could not fit — window too short for a slope)"
elif kappa <= 0.25:
    qual = "  (σ-stable)"
    kline = f"  κ≈{kappa:.2f} (σ-stable — 2nd moment converged; the standardized gap is trustworthy)"
elif kappa <= 0.5:
    qual = "  (σ pre-asymptotic — verdict tentative)"
    kline = f"  κ≈{kappa:.2f} (σ pre-asymptotic — 2nd moment converging slowly; verdict tentative)"
else:
    qual = f"  (fat-tailed: n={n} likely too few — σ-standardized gap UNRELIABLE)"
    kline = f"  κ≈{kappa:.2f} (fat-tailed: LLN needs ≫n={n} for σ to converge — σ-standardized gap UNRELIABLE)"

# excess kurtosis, reported as the familiar cross-check (cosh-gap and kurtosis both rank tails)
m = sum(xs) / n
var = sum((x - m) ** 2 for x in xs) / n
sd = math.sqrt(var) if var > 0 else 0.0
kurt = (sum(((x - m) / sd) ** 4 for x in xs) / n - 3.0) if sd > 0 else 0.0

print(f"convexity: {verdict}   (Jensen-gap Δ vs matched Gaussian){qual}")
print(f"  n={n}  λ={lam}  mean={m:.4g}  sd={sd:.4g}  excess-kurtosis={kurt:+.3f}")
print(f"  G_obs={g_obs:.4f}  G_ref(Gaussian)={g_ref:.4f}  Δ={delta:+.4f}  95%CI=[{lo:+.4f}, {hi:+.4f}]")
print(kline)
print(f"  → {gloss}")
print(f"  tail-direction: {a_verdict}   A(L−R)={a_obs:+.4f}  95%CI=[{a_lo:+.4f}, {a_hi:+.4f}]  (Taleb-Douady fragility side)")
print(f"    → {a_gloss}")

# ── THRESHOLD-STABILITY GATE (arXiv:2606.16511, threshold-fishing trap) ───────────────────────
# Both verdicts above are read off ONE arbitrary λ. Re-run them over a multiplicative neighbourhood
# of that λ and require the claim to hold across it — the paper's q∈[0.95,0.99] sweep, transferred to
# this tool's own free knob. Report-only: it qualifies the headline, it never overrules it.
def verdicts_at(lam_i, rng_i):
    """Full bootstrap verdict pair at a SWEPT λ (same estimator, same B, own RNG stream)."""
    gr_i = math.exp(lam_i * lam_i / 2.0) - 1.0
    ds, az = [], []
    for _ in range(B):
        rs = [xs[rng_i.randrange(n)] for _ in range(n)]
        ds.append(gap(rs, lam_i) - gr_i)
        az.append(asymmetry(rs, lam_i))
    ds.sort(); az.sort()
    d_lo, d_hi = ds[int(0.025 * B)], ds[int(0.975 * B)]
    z_lo, z_hi = az[int(0.025 * B)], az[int(0.975 * B)]
    v = "CONVEX" if d_lo > 0 else ("ROBUST" if d_hi < 0 else "GAUSSIAN-LIKE")
    a = "LEFT-HEAVY" if z_lo > 0 else ("RIGHT-HEAVY" if z_hi < 0 else "SYMMETRIC")
    # r = G_obs/G_ref is the SCALE-FREE companion figure: Δ's units explode with λ, this does not.
    r = (gap(xs, lam_i) / gr_i) if gr_i > 0 else float("nan")
    return v, a, r

mults = []
for tok in os.environ.get("MESH_CVX_LAMBDAS", "0.5,0.7071,1.0,1.4142,2.0").split(","):
    try:
        mu = float(tok)
    except ValueError:
        continue
    if mu > 0:
        mults.append(mu)
if not mults:
    mults = [0.5, 0.7071, 1.0, 1.4142, 2.0]

# Own RNG stream (seed+1), constructed AFTER κ has drawn, so every pre-existing number above is
# byte-identical to before this gate existed.
rng_s = random.Random(seed + 1)
grid = []
for mu in mults:
    lam_i = lam * mu
    if abs(mu - 1.0) < 1e-12:      # the anchor: reuse its B=2000 result, do not redraw
        r_anchor = (g_obs / g_ref) if g_ref > 0 else float("nan")
        grid.append((lam_i, verdict, a_verdict, r_anchor, True))
    else:
        v, a, r = verdicts_at(lam_i, rng_s)
        grid.append((lam_i, v, a, r, False))

flips = [(l_i, v, a) for (l_i, v, a, _r, _anc) in grid if v != verdict or a != a_verdict]
if len(grid) < 2:
    s_verdict = "n/a"
    s_gloss = "λ-sweep needs ≥2 points (check MESH_CVX_LAMBDAS) — the threshold gate did not run"
elif not flips:
    s_verdict = "STABLE"
    s_gloss = (f"both verdicts hold across λ∈[{grid[0][0]:.2f}, {grid[-1][0]:.2f}] — the claim is a "
               "property of the data, not of the default λ")
else:
    s_verdict = "THRESHOLD-FRAGILE"
    named = "; ".join(
        f"λ={l_i:.2f}: " + " & ".join(
            ([f"{verdict}→{v}"] if v != verdict else []) + ([f"{a_verdict}→{a}"] if a != a_verdict else []))
        for (l_i, v, a) in flips)
    s_gloss = (f"the verdict CHANGES inside its own knob's neighbourhood ({named}) — the claim at "
               f"λ={lam} sits within the estimator's noise envelope; treat it as UNGATED")

print(f"  threshold-stability: {s_verdict}   (λ-sweep gate, arXiv:2606.16511)")
print("    λ-sweep: " + " · ".join(
    (f"[{l_i:.2f}→{v}/{a} r={r:.2f}]" if anc else f"{l_i:.2f}→{v}/{a} r={r:.2f}")
    for (l_i, v, a, r, anc) in grid) + "   ([·]=anchor)")
print(f"    → {s_gloss}")
PY
}

if [ "$MODE" = "test" ]; then
  echo "── mutation-verify: fat-tailed→CONVEX, bounded→ROBUST (RED→GREEN gate) ──"
  # Fixture A: a heavy-tailed series (mostly ~0, rare large spikes) — must read CONVEX.
  fat="$(python3 - <<'PY'
import random
r = random.Random(42)
out = []
for i in range(200):
    out.append(str(round(r.gauss(0, 1), 4)) if r.random() > 0.06 else str(round(r.gauss(0, 12), 4)))
print("\n".join(out))
PY
)"
  # Fixture B: a bounded/uniform series (thin tails) — must read ROBUST.
  flat="$(python3 - <<'PY'
import random
r = random.Random(7)
print("\n".join(str(round(r.uniform(-1, 1), 4)) for _ in range(200)))
PY
)"
  echo "[fat-tailed fixture]"
  fo="$(printf '%s\n' "$fat" | MESH_CVX_SEED=1729 _compute "$LAMBDA")"; echo "$fo"
  echo "$fo" | grep -q 'convexity: CONVEX' \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (heavy-tailed series not detected as CONVEX)"; exit 1; }
  echo "[bounded fixture]"
  bo="$(printf '%s\n' "$flat" | MESH_CVX_SEED=1729 _compute "$LAMBDA")"; echo "$bo"
  echo "$bo" | grep -q 'convexity: ROBUST' \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (bounded uniform series not detected as ROBUST)"; exit 1; }

  echo "── κ pre-check: Cauchy→UNRELIABLE, bounded→σ-stable (RED→GREEN gate) ──"
  # Fixture C: a standard Cauchy series (α=1, infinite variance) — σ NEVER converges. Must read
  # κ→1 and carry the UNRELIABLE tag. This is the ONLY fixture that exercises κ: the fat-tailed
  # mixture above has FINITE variance (leptokurtic but M(b)∝√b), so κ alone cannot be asserted on it.
  cauchy="$(python3 - <<'PY'
import random, math
r = random.Random(99)
print("\n".join(str(round(math.tan(math.pi * (r.random() - 0.5)), 4)) for _ in range(200)))
PY
)"
  echo "[cauchy fixture]"
  co="$(printf '%s\n' "$cauchy" | MESH_CVX_SEED=1729 _compute "$LAMBDA")"; echo "$co"
  echo "$co" | grep -q 'UNRELIABLE' \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (infinite-variance Cauchy not flagged κ-UNRELIABLE — σ-standardized verdict trusted on a divergent 2nd moment)"; exit 1; }
  # The bounded fixture's σ DOES converge — its κ must read σ-stable (thin-tailed, β≈½).
  echo "$bo" | grep -q '(σ-stable)' \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (bounded fixture not read as σ-stable — κ cannot separate thin from fat)"; exit 1; }

  echo "── tail-direction: left-skewed spikes→LEFT-HEAVY, symmetric→SYMMETRIC (RED→GREEN gate) ──"
  # Fixture D: small noise with rare large NEGATIVE spikes — convex weight sits in the DOWN tail.
  # Must read LEFT-HEAVY. The symmetric fat fixture (A) has spikes BOTH ways → must read SYMMETRIC,
  # so a vacuous "always LEFT-HEAVY" cannot pass both: the two GREENs cannot coexist unless the
  # split at z=0 is real. This is the axis that answers the CAFE verdict's punted sign.
  leftheavy="$(python3 - <<'PY'
import random
r = random.Random(11)
out = []
for i in range(200):
    out.append(str(round(r.gauss(0, 1), 4)) if r.random() > 0.06 else str(round(r.gauss(-14, 3), 4)))
print("\n".join(out))
PY
)"
  echo "[left-skewed fixture]"
  lo_out="$(printf '%s\n' "$leftheavy" | MESH_CVX_SEED=1729 _compute "$LAMBDA")"; echo "$lo_out"
  echo "$lo_out" | grep -q 'tail-direction: LEFT-HEAVY' \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (down-tail spikes not detected as LEFT-HEAVY — fragility direction blind)"; exit 1; }
  # The symmetric fat fixture must NOT read directional — its convex weight is balanced.
  echo "$fo" | grep -q 'tail-direction: SYMMETRIC' \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (symmetric fat fixture read as directional — the z=0 split is vacuous)"; exit 1; }

  echo "── threshold-stability: knob-fragile claim→THRESHOLD-FRAGILE, robust claim→STABLE (RED→GREEN gate) ──"
  # Fixture E: n=80, 5% spikes at σ=5 — reads CONVEX at the default λ=1.0 but the claim EVAPORATES to
  # GAUSSIAN-LIKE at λ=2.0. The paper's threshold-fishing trap in this tool's own coordinates: a PASS
  # that lives at one point of a free knob. Must read THRESHOLD-FRAGILE.
  knobfrag="$(python3 - <<'PY'
import random
r = random.Random(11)
out = []
for i in range(80):
    out.append(str(round(r.gauss(0, 1), 4)) if r.random() > 0.05 else str(round(r.gauss(0, 5), 4)))
print("\n".join(out))
PY
)"
  echo "[knob-fragile fixture]"
  kf="$(printf '%s\n' "$knobfrag" | MESH_CVX_SEED=1729 _compute "$LAMBDA")"; echo "$kf"
  echo "$kf" | grep -q 'threshold-stability: THRESHOLD-FRAGILE' \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a CONVEX that flips to GAUSSIAN-LIKE inside its own λ-neighbourhood was not flagged — the threshold gate is vacuous)"; exit 1; }
  echo "$kf" | grep -q 'λ=2.00: CONVEX→GAUSSIAN-LIKE' \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (THRESHOLD-FRAGILE printed without NAMING the λ that flips — an unnamed flip is not actionable)"; exit 1; }
  # Fixture F: n=60, 3% spikes at σ=6 — the Δ verdict never moves; the DIRECTION claim (RIGHT-HEAVY)
  # exists only at λ≥1.0. Proves the gate covers BOTH published verdicts, not just the headline one.
  dirfrag="$(python3 - <<'PY'
import random
r = random.Random(21)
out = []
for i in range(60):
    out.append(str(round(r.gauss(0, 1), 4)) if r.random() > 0.03 else str(round(r.gauss(0, 6), 4)))
print("\n".join(out))
PY
)"
  echo "[direction-fragile fixture]"
  df="$(printf '%s\n' "$dirfrag" | MESH_CVX_SEED=1729 _compute "$LAMBDA")"; echo "$df"
  echo "$df" | grep -q 'threshold-stability: THRESHOLD-FRAGILE' \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a knob-dependent tail-DIRECTION claim was not flagged — the gate watches only the headline verdict)"; exit 1; }
  echo "$df" | grep -q 'RIGHT-HEAVY→SYMMETRIC' \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (direction flip not named — the gate saw the Δ axis only)"; exit 1; }
  # κ is NOT a substitute: this fixture's κ says the 2nd moment converged and the gap is trustworthy,
  # while the λ-sweep destroys the direction claim. If these ever agree by construction the gate is a
  # duplicate of the κ pre-check and buys nothing.
  echo "$df" | grep -q '(σ-stable' \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (direction-fragile fixture no longer reads σ-stable — the orthogonality of κ and the λ-gate is no longer demonstrated)"; exit 1; }
  # The 200-sample fat fixture's CONVEX survives the whole neighbourhood — a vacuous "always
  # THRESHOLD-FRAGILE" cannot pass this and the two above at once.
  echo "$fo" | grep -q 'threshold-stability: STABLE' \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a claim that holds across the full λ-neighbourhood read as fragile — the gate flags everything)"; exit 1; }

  echo "smoke-test: ok (fat-tailed→CONVEX, bounded→ROBUST, Cauchy→κ-UNRELIABLE, left-spikes→LEFT-HEAVY, knob-fragile→THRESHOLD-FRAGILE; verdicts+qualifiers DISTINCT)"
  echo "(mutation check A: flip the verdict rule to key on the MEAN instead of Δ — both fixtures"
  echo " have mean≈0 → both collapse to one verdict → the two GREENs cannot both hold → RED.)"
  echo "(mutation check B: feed the Cauchy fixture but assert the UNRELIABLE tag is ABSENT — a κ"
  echo " estimator that reads σ-stable on infinite variance is vacuous → the assertion goes RED.)"
  echo "(mutation check C: drop the −gR from the asymmetry split (weight only the down tail) — the"
  echo " symmetric fat fixture then reads LEFT-HEAVY, contradicting its SYMMETRIC GREEN → RED. Verified.)"
  echo "(mutation check D: pin the λ-sweep to the anchor alone (MESH_CVX_LAMBDAS=1.0) — with no"
  echo " neighbourhood there is nothing to flip; both fragile fixtures read n/a → RED. Verified, rc=1.)"
  echo "(mutation check E: flips:=[] (the sweep never compared to the anchor) — everything reads"
  echo " STABLE and both fragile fixtures go RED while the fat one stays green: the three GREENs"
  echo " cannot coexist unless the sweep really re-estimates at each λ. Verified, rc=1.)"
  echo "(mutation check F: make STABLE unreachable (always flag) — the 200-sample fat fixture, whose"
  echo " CONVEX genuinely holds across the whole neighbourhood, goes RED. Verified, rc=1. NB a cruder"
  echo " always-fragile mutant (flips:=grid[:1]) also goes RED but at the NAMING assertion first, not"
  echo " this one — it is red for a different reason, so F is the assertion that actually guards STABLE.)"
  exit 0
fi

_compute "$LAMBDA"
